  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Harmonic Frequencies",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute harmonic frequencies as integer multiples of the fundamental frequency and interpret each multiple as a sinusoidal component in the Fourier-series frequency-domain view.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonic Frequencies",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Harmonic Frequencies",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Once the fundamental frequency $\\omega_0$ is known, harmonic frequencies are the positive integer multiples of that same spacing. In the Fourier-series frequency-domain view, those multiples are the discrete frequencies you read off as separate sinusoidal components.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_k = k\\omega_0, \\quad k = 1,2,3,\\dots",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $k$ is the harmonic index, and it is a positive integer. Start with $k=1$ so the first harmonic is just $\\omega_0$, then use the same rule for $k=2$, $k=3$, and beyond.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_1 = \\omega_0, \\quad \\omega_2 = 2\\omega_0, \\quad \\omega_3 = 3\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This gives a simple ladder of equally spaced harmonic frequencies: each new harmonic is one more copy of the fundamental spacing. The pattern is always the same rule, multiply $\\omega_0$ by the integer index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So when you look at a Fourier-series spectrum, treat each nonzero harmonic line as one sinusoidal component at $\\omega_k = k\\omega_0$. The $0$-frequency DC term is separate from the harmonic frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonic Frequencies",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing Harmonic Frequencies from a Fundamental Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $\\omega_0 = 3\\pi$ rad/s and $k = 4$, compute the fourth harmonic frequency.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the integer harmonic index $k$. Use the rule from the introduction, $\\omega_k = k\\omega_0$, and substitute the given values for $k$ and $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_4 = 4\\omega_0 = 4(3\\pi) = 12\\pi\\,\\text{rad/s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fourth harmonic frequency is $12\\pi$ rad/s. The only operation is multiplying the fundamental frequency by the harmonic index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonic Frequencies",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Listing the First Several Harmonic Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "List the first five harmonic frequencies when $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi$ rad/s.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the request to list several harmonics. Use the same rule from the previous example, $\\omega_k = k\\omega_0$, and start with $k=1$ so the list begins at the first harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\omega_1 &= 1(2\\pi) = 2\\pi\\,\\text{rad/s}\\\\\\omega_2 &= 2(2\\pi) = 4\\pi\\,\\text{rad/s}\\\\\\omega_3 &= 3(2\\pi) = 6\\pi\\,\\text{rad/s}\\\\\\omega_4 &= 4(2\\pi) = 8\\pi\\,\\text{rad/s}\\\\\\omega_5 &= 5(2\\pi) = 10\\pi\\,\\text{rad/s}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the first five harmonic frequencies are $2\\pi$, $4\\pi$, $6\\pi$, $8\\pi$, and $10\\pi$ rad/s in ascending order. Each entry is an integer multiple of the same fundamental frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonic Frequencies",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Matching Frequencies to Harmonic Indices",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $\\omega_0 = 5\\pi$ rad/s, which harmonic index corresponds to $20\\pi$ rad/s?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the target frequency $20\\pi$ rad/s. To match a frequency to a harmonic index, compare it to the spacing frequency $\\omega_0$ and form the ratio $\\omega / \\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega_0} = \\frac{20\\pi}{5\\pi} = 4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The ratio is the integer $4$, so the frequency is exactly $4\\omega_0$. The matching harmonic index is $k=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonic Frequencies",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Harmonics as Frequency-Domain Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A Fourier-series spectrum has lines at $\\omega_0$, $3\\omega_0$, and $5\\omega_0$. Which harmonic components are present, and what do they mean?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a set of spectral lines at integer multiples of $\\omega_0$. In the Fourier-series frequency-domain view, each line is one sinusoidal component at a harmonic frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\omega_0 &\\rightarrow k=1 \\\\\n3\\omega_0 &\\rightarrow k=3 \\\\\n5\\omega_0 &\\rightarrow k=5\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the spectrum contains the 1st, 3rd, and 5th harmonic components. Each allowed line marks one sinusoidal component in the harmonic decomposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
